From: SparkEngine Wine Compatibility <spark-wine@example.invalid>
Date: Tue, 14 Apr 2026 00:00:01 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] ntdll: refresh thread_stack_info from pthread bounds
 before declaring stack overflow

virtual_setup_exception() range-checks the address at which it is
about to write the Windows exception frame against DeallocationStack
and StackBase cached in the TEB at thread creation time. When the
proposed frame would land in the first 4 KB of the cached mapping
(the guard region), it calls abort_thread() with an "unrecoverable
stack overflow" error and kills the thread.

On conventional Linux kernels the TEB bounds exactly reflect the
mapping the pthread's stack actually occupies, so the check is
correct. On user-mode Linux kernels this is not always the case:

  * gVisor's runsc sentry places pthread stacks through its own Go
    allocator and does not necessarily hand Wine a mapping that
    starts at the DeallocationStack the TEB caches.
  * Firecracker and some sandbox runtimes exhibit similar drift
    between Wine's bookkeeping and the kernel's view.
  * Containerised setups with unusual mount/mmap_min_addr tuning
    can produce the same result.

The visible symptom after applying the companion patch that fixes
segv_handler's zero-trap loop (patch 1/2 in this series) is a
"stack overflow 64 bytes" ERR followed by a silent abort_thread()
on the first exception the guest raises, even though the underlying
pthread stack has megabytes of headroom.

Ask the kernel directly via pthread_getattr_np() +
pthread_attr_getstack() before aborting. If the kernel reports a
start address below the cached DeallocationStack, widen stack_info
and retry the range check. On bare metal the kernel's view and the
cached TEB view agree, so this path is a no-op. On gVisor and
similar environments the widened bounds let the exception frame be
written into genuinely mapped, writable memory.

Only take the refresh path when the cached bounds are about to
reject the frame; the happy path is unchanged. Guarded with
__linux__ && __GLIBC__ because pthread_getattr_np() is a glibc
extension; on other C libraries and non-Linux targets the helper is
a no-op and behavior is unchanged.

Signed-off-by: SparkEngine Wine Compatibility <spark-wine@example.invalid>
---
 dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c | 76 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++------
 1 file changed, 65 insertions(+), 11 deletions(-)

diff --git a/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c b/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c
index abe1b4d..15b24c8 100644
--- a/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c
@@ -4018,6 +4018,53 @@ NTSTATUS virtual_handle_fault( void *addr, DWORD err, void *stack )
 }


+/***********************************************************************
+ *           refresh_stack_info_from_pthread
+ *
+ * Some environments (gVisor's runsc sentry, user-mode Linux kernels,
+ * Firecracker, certain seccomp/mount namespace layouts) allocate the
+ * pthread stack at an address range that does not exactly match the
+ * DeallocationStack/StackBase bounds cached in the TEB at thread
+ * creation time. When the TEB bounds look too narrow (e.g. because
+ * the active pthread stack lives at a lower address than
+ * DeallocationStack), virtual_setup_exception can reject a legitimate
+ * exception frame as a stack overflow and kill the thread.
+ *
+ * Query the real pthread stack bounds from the kernel via
+ * pthread_getattr_np(). If the kernel reports a lower start address
+ * than the cached TEB bounds, widen stack_info->start so the caller
+ * can finish writing the exception frame into memory that is in fact
+ * mapped and writable. On all conventional Linux kernels the pthread
+ * bounds and the TEB bounds agree, and this helper is a no-op.
+ *
+ * Returns TRUE if stack_info->start was widened.
+ */
+static BOOL refresh_stack_info_from_pthread( struct thread_stack_info *stack_info )
+{
+#if defined(__linux__) && defined(__GLIBC__)
+    pthread_attr_t attr;
+    void *addr;
+    size_t sz;
+    BOOL ret = FALSE;
+
+    if (pthread_getattr_np( pthread_self(), &attr )) return FALSE;
+    if (!pthread_attr_getstack( &attr, &addr, &sz ))
+    {
+        if ((char *)addr < stack_info->start)
+        {
+            stack_info->start = (char *)addr;
+            ret = TRUE;
+        }
+    }
+    pthread_attr_destroy( &attr );
+    return ret;
+#else
+    (void)stack_info;
+    return FALSE;
+#endif
+}
+
+
 /***********************************************************************
  *           virtual_setup_exception
  */
@@ -4043,11 +4090,28 @@ void *virtual_setup_exception( void *stack_ptr, size_t size, EXCEPTION_RECORD *r

     if (stack < stack_info.start + 4096)
     {
-        /* stack overflow on last page, unrecoverable */
-        UINT diff = stack_info.start + 4096 - stack;
-        ERR( "stack overflow %u bytes addr %p stack %p (%p-%p-%p)\n",
-             diff, rec->ExceptionAddress, stack, stack_info.start, stack_info.limit, stack_info.end );
-        abort_thread(1);
+        /* The proposed exception frame would land in the guard page of
+         * the thread stack as known to the TEB. Before declaring this an
+         * unrecoverable overflow, give the kernel one chance to correct
+         * our bookkeeping: under user-mode Linux kernels (gVisor,
+         * Firecracker, etc.) the actual pthread stack may extend below
+         * the cached DeallocationStack. */
+        if (refresh_stack_info_from_pthread( &stack_info ) &&
+            stack >= stack_info.start + 4096)
+        {
+            WARN( "stack_info start widened from TEB value to pthread value;"
+                  " proceeding with exception frame addr %p stack %p (%p-%p-%p)\n",
+                  rec->ExceptionAddress, stack, stack_info.start,
+                  stack_info.limit, stack_info.end );
+        }
+        else
+        {
+            /* stack overflow on last page, unrecoverable */
+            UINT diff = stack_info.start + 4096 - stack;
+            ERR( "stack overflow %u bytes addr %p stack %p (%p-%p-%p)\n",
+                 diff, rec->ExceptionAddress, stack, stack_info.start, stack_info.limit, stack_info.end );
+            abort_thread(1);
+        }
     }
     else if (stack < stack_info.limit)
     {
